libhsakmt fmm.c, gfx803: take out the mapping that
va-reuse-defer-mapping.patch restores for a parked object, and unmap a
parked object before fmm_do_release() frees its BO. A parked object that is
still GPU-mapped when it ages out leaves a kernel mapping behind at a VA
that the fmm hands out again, and every code-object load placed there then
fails with HSA_STATUS_ERROR_OUT_OF_RESOURCES.

WHY
---
amdgpu_amdkfd_gpuvm_free_memory_of_gpu refuses to free a BO that is still
GPU-mapped and returns EBUSY. When fmm_do_release() frees a parked object
that is still mapped, the BO and its kernel mapping survive, while
fmm_do_release() still returns the area to the aperture free-list and drops
the vm_object. The fmm then places a new allocation in that range, and the
kernel rejects the new map as an overlap (dmesg: amdgpu_vm_bo_map
"va ... conflict with ..."). The restored mapping of
va-reuse-defer-mapping.patch makes every parked object such a case.

vLLM (torch 2.14) on gfx803 shows it as a SIGSEGV on its first kernel
launch. executable_load_agent_code_object fails, because the kernel rejects
the GPUVM map of the code BO with EINVAL, and that surfaces as
HSA_STATUS_ERROR_OUT_OF_RESOURCES. GetFunc then returns
hipErrorNoBinaryForGpu, and the launch fallback in ihipLaunchKernel
reinterpret_casts the host stub as a hipFunction_t, which crashes in
amd::Kernel::getDeviceKernel.

The same EBUSY orphans each such BO until process exit. A plain
hipMalloc/hipFree loop, with no framework, then never returns memory to
hipMemGetInfo or mem_info_vram_used
(https://github.com/Schaka/rocm-gfx803/issues/2#issuecomment-5569269314).

Without the restored mapping, a parked object keeps its VA reserved and the
collision is gone. The cost is the tolerance that the restored mapping gives
a late GPU access to a parked VA. The unmap in fmm_do_release() covers any
parked object that is still mapped when it ages out, however it got there.

Freed VRAM shows up as reclaimed only if reserved_aperture_release() drops
the CPU mapping of that VA range on its own release path, as stock does. With
a full Resizable BAR every local-VRAM allocation is CPU-accessible, so
hipMalloc's aperture goes through that function too.

WHAT
----
- Remove the _fmm_map_to_gpu() call and its comment from the park branch of
  __fmm_release_locked().
- In fmm_do_release(), unmap the object through _fmm_unmap_from_gpu()
  before the BO free. That helper builds kfd_ioctl_unmap_memory_from_gpu_args
  from the handle and object->mapped_device_id_array, checks the ioctl
  result per handle, and issues no ioctl for an object that has no mapped
  devices.

Applies to: rocm-systems release/therock-10.2, commit
43fa692e568e65a1fb84f2d1c74415b042839f10, after
va-reuse-defer-mapping.patch, whose _fmm_map_to_gpu() call it removes.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (gfx803 box, 2026-09-02) on ROCm 10.0: with this
patch and d2h-null-dsthost.patch, the vLLM Qwen3.5-2B bench
(qwen35_2b_bench_v3.py) completes with EXIT=0, prefill 311.0 tok/s and
decode 30.2 tok/s, on a 2101-token prompt and 128 decode tokens. Every
deferred BO unmaps and frees: AMDKFD_IOC_FREE_MEMORY_OF_GPU returns 0 for
every handle, checked with strace.

NOT YET RE-VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE on ROCm 10.2. To verify, in an image
built from the commit above: qwen35_2b_bench_v3.py must complete with
EXIT=0; a hipMalloc/hipFree loop must bring mem_info_vram_used back to its
starting value; dmesg must show no amdgpu_vm_bo_map conflict; and the torch
`x.double().cpu()` alloc/free churn loop from va-reuse-defer-mapping.patch
must run without a GPU page fault.

diff --git a/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c b/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
index a9a9e95992..0d4f029a83 100644
--- a/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
+++ b/projects/rocr-runtime/libhsakmt/src/fmm.c
@@ -1214,6 +1214,16 @@ static void fmm_do_release(HsaKFDContext *ctx, struct deferred_release *e)
 	struct kfd_ioctl_free_memory_of_gpu_args args = {0};
 	uint32_t i;
 
+	/* free_memory_of_gpu refuses a BO that is still GPU-mapped with EBUSY,
+	 * which orphans the BO until process exit while this function still
+	 * returns its VA to the free-list. Unmap first, through
+	 * _fmm_unmap_from_gpu(), which builds the real
+	 * kfd_ioctl_unmap_memory_from_gpu_args (handle plus
+	 * object->mapped_device_id_array) and issues no ioctl for an object that
+	 * has no mapped devices.
+	 */
+	_fmm_unmap_from_gpu(ctx, e->aperture, e->object->start, NULL, 0, e->object);
+
 	for (i = 0; i < e->object->handle_num; i++) {
 		args.handle = e->object->handles[i];
 		if (!args.handle)
@@ -3321,18 +3331,6 @@ static int __fmm_release_locked(HsaKFDContext *ctx,
 	    !object->is_svm_paged &&
 	    aperture->ops != &mmap_aperture_ops) {
 		object->deferred = true;
-		/* KfdDriver::FreeMemory (the hsa-runtime) calls
-		 * hsaKmtUnmapMemoryToGPU before it calls hsaKmtFreeMemory, so
-		 * by the time a free reaches the defer here its GPU mapping is
-		 * already gone (mapping_count == 0, PTEs removed). Re-establish
-		 * it, so that a lagging GPU access to the freed buffer hits the
-		 * parked pages instead of faulting on a reserved but unmapped VA
-		 * (stock tolerates such an access through near-instant VA+phys
-		 * reuse).
-		 */
-		if (object->mapping_count == 0)
-			_fmm_map_to_gpu(ctx, aperture, object->start,
-					object->size, object, NULL, 0);
 		fmm_defer_release(ctx, aperture, object);
 		return 0;
 	}
